</td><td width="4" align="left" valign="top"><IMG alt="" border=0 height=1 src="/images/blank.gif" width=4></td><td width="100%" align="left" valign="top"><font face="Arial" size="+1" color="black"><b>Warrior Concept Art</b></font><br><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="black">This piece of art was released by Origin as an indication of the look and feel of a specific area in UO2 but was NOT taken in the UO2 engine; this is a rendered piece of art just to demonstrate the type of architecture and denizen present in UO2.<p>If we examine the image, we seen a warrior atop a rather large mount (note the large stubby tail coming up behind the warrior).  The warrior is brandishing a bardiche in his right hand and his left hand appears to be a very large claw extended toward the camera with serrations near the end.<p>In the background, a large stone and metal structure looms over the mounted warrior and a small flying creature hovers on the left side of the image.</font></P></td></tr></table>
<p>
<table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
        <TBODY>
<tr>
    <td width="319" align="left" valign="top">
	
	<table  border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" align="left" bgcolor="black">
	<tr>
		<td width="100%"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
	<tr>
		<td bgcolor="#e0dec5"><IMG alt="" border=0 height=237 src="/images/pic1.jpg" width=319></td>
</tr>
</table>

</td>
</tr>
</table>
</td><td width="4" align="left" valign="top"><IMG alt="" border=0 height=1 src="/images/blank.gif" width=4></td><td width="100%" align="left" valign="top"><font face="Arial" size="+1" color="black"><b>The First Glimpse&nbsp;of the UO2 Engine?</b></font><br><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="black">It is unknown whether these images represent concept art or are actually still images from the world of Britannia as seen through the UO2 engine.  The source of these images is the <a class=post href="http://www.mcfarlane.com/toyfair2000/showroomtour/tour.select.html" 
           >McFarlane Toys Toyfair 2000 Tour</a>.  The images seen here were taken from the QTVR version of the tour.<p>In this first image, we see a hulking beast 
            in the foreground while a castle and rolling hills decorate the 
            background.&nbsp; This creature is an unknown entity but is 
            apparently a very large humanoid wrapped in a chain.&nbsp; From the 
            left part of his chest surges forth a second head, this one 
            appearing much more ominous than the one atop its neck.&nbsp; His 
            right arm appears fairly normal while his left is anything 
            but.&nbsp; Seeming to be a long almost metallic appendage, this 
            creature is obviously not something to be trifled with. </font></p>



</td></tr></table>
<p>
<table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
        
<tr>
    <td width="237" align="left" valign="top">
	
	<table  border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" align="left" bgcolor="black">
	<tr>
		<td width="100%"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
	<tr>
		<td bgcolor="#e0dec5"><IMG alt="" border=0 height=237 src="/images/pic6.jpg" width=313></td>
</tr>
</table>

</td>
</tr>
</table>
</td><td width="4" align="left" valign="top"><IMG alt="" border=0 height=1 src="/images/blank.gif" width=4></td><td width="100%" align="left" valign="top"><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="black">This is a MUCH 
            closer view of the monstrosity in the previous image.&nbsp; This is 
            definately not how you would like to meet up with this creature in 
            UO2.&nbsp; You can more easily see the chain wrapping the beast in 
            this image.&nbsp; Also, the head springing forth from his chest is 
            actually lined with a metallic rim.&nbsp; His left arm still seems 
            to be some sort of either greyish scaled appendage or, more likely, 
            it is a metallic arm. </font>
            </td></tr></table></p>
			<table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
        
<tr>
    <td width="239" align="left" valign="top">
	
	<table  border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" align="left" bgcolor="black">
	<tr>
		<td width="100%"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
	<tr>
		<td bgcolor="#e0dec5"><IMG alt="" border=0 height=239 src="/images/pic2.jpg" width=295></td>
</tr>
</table>

</td>
</tr>
</table>
</td><td width="4" align="left" valign="top"><IMG alt="" border=0 height=1 src="/images/blank.gif" width=4></td><td width="100%" align="left" valign="top">
            <P><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="black">    
               In this image we see people dancing.&nbsp; If 
            you take a very close look at the figure on the right, you 
            will notice that he is indeed the Warrior from the concept art!&nbsp; 
            His armor clearly gives him away but notice that he doesn't 
            sport the stylish claw of the concept art.&nbsp; Hmm...are these appendages 
            such as the one on Blackthorn actually removable and serve more as 
            weapons than replacements for limbs? </font></P>
            <P><FONT face=Arial size=2>Also, notice the ears on the female 
            character&nbsp;to the left.&nbsp; Don't they appear pointed if they 
            are indeed ears?&nbsp; Could this be our first glimpse at the 
            Meer?&nbsp; Or considering the technology of the concept art, maybe 
            these are Juka.&nbsp; Or, perhaps this is the first case of a Meer 
            dancing with a Juka!</FONT></P></td></tr></table>
			<P></P>
			<table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
        
<tr>
    <td width="236" align="left" valign="top">
	
	<table  border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" align="left" bgcolor="black">
	<tr>
		<td width="100%"><table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
	<tr>
		<td bgcolor="#e0dec5"><IMG alt="" border=0 height=236 src="/images/pic3.jpg" width=266></td>
</tr>
